  "text": "To find the sum of the y-coordinates of the intersection points between the parabola \\(x = 34y^2\\) and the function \\(f(x)\\), we need to solve for the values of \\(y\\) where the two equations intersect. We'll first determine the values of \\(x\\) where \\(f(x) = 34y^2\\), and then find the corresponding \\(y\\) values for each of those \\(x\\) values.\n\nThe function \\(f(x)\\) is defined piecewise, so we need to consider the intervals \\([-1, 1)\\) and \\([1, 3)\\) separately.\n\n### Step 1: Solve for \\(x\\) in the intervals\n\n1. For \\(x \\in [-1, 1)\\):\n\\[ f(x) = x \\]\n\\[ x = 34y^2 \\]\n\n2. For \\(x \\in [1, 3)\\):\n\\[ f(x) = 2 - x \\]\n\\[ 2 - x = 34y^2 \\]\n\\[ x = 2 - 34y^2 \\]\n\n### Step 2: Determine the valid \\(x\\) values for each interval\n\n1. For \\(x \\in [-1, 1)\\):\n\\[ -1 \\leq x < 1 \\]\n\\[ -1 \\leq 34y^2 < 1 \\]\n\\[ -\\frac{1}{34} \\leq y^2 < \\frac{1}{34} \\]\n\\[ -\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{34}} < y < \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{34}} \\]\n\n2. For \\(x \\in [1, 3)\\):\n\\[ 1 \\leq x < 3 \\]\n\\[ 1 \\leq 2 - 34y^2 < 3 \\]\n\\[ -1 \\leq -34y^2 < 1 \\]\n\\[ -\\frac{1}{34} < y^2 \\leq \\frac{1}{34} \\]\n\\[ -\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{34}} < y < \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{34}} \\]\n\n### Step 3: Solve for \\(y\\) in each interval\n\n1. For \\(x \\in [-1, 1)\\):\n\\[ x = 34y^2 \\]\n\\[ y^2 = \\frac{x}{34} \\]\n\\[ y = \\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{x}{34}} \\]\nSince \\(x \\in [-1, 1)\\), we have:\n\\[ y = \\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{-1}{34}} \\text{ (not valid since } y^2 \\text{ must be non-negative)} \\]\n\\[ y = \\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{34}} \\]\n\n2. For \\(x \\in [1, 3)\\):\n\\[ x = 2 - 34y^2 \\]\n\\[ 34y^2 = 2 - x \\]\n\\[ y^2 = \\frac{2 - x}{34} \\]\n\\[ y = \\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{2 - x}{34}} \\]\nSince \\(x \\in [1, 3)\\), we have:\n\\[ y = \\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{2 - 1}{34}} \\]\n\\[ y = \\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{34}} \\]\n\\[ y = \\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{2 - 3}{34}} \\text{ (not valid since } 2 - 3 < 0) \\]\n\n### Step 4: Sum the valid \\(y\\) values\n\nThe valid \\(y\\) values are:\n\\[ y = \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{34}} \\]\n\\[ y = -\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{34}} \\]\n\\[ y = \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{34}} \\]\n\\[ y = -\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{34}} \\]\n\nThe sum of the \\(y\\)-coordinates is:\n\\[ \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{34}} + \\left(-\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{34}}\\right) + \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{34}} + \\left(-\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{34}}\\right) = 0 \\]\n\nHowever, we need to express the answer in the form \\(\\frac{a + b\\sqrt{c}}{d}\\). Since the sum is 0, we can write it as:\n\\[ 0 = \\frac{0 + 0\\sqrt{1}}{1} \\]\n\nThus, \\(a = 0\\), \\(b = 0\\), \\(c = 1\\), and \\(d = 1\\).\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\[ \\boxed{2} \\]",
